---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import McpExplorer from '../components/McpExplorer';
import DiagramFigure from '../components/DiagramFigure.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import MaintainerOnly from '../components/MaintainerOnly.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="MCP playground"
  description="Explore the Model Context Protocol servers that power AI-assisted Atelier UI development — Storybook, Figma, and Nx integrations."
  currentPath="/mcp"
  width="wide"
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero title="MCP playground" ledeMax="620px">
      Explore the Model Context Protocol servers that power AI-assisted Atelier
      UI development — one Storybook MCP per framework plus the
      figma-console-mcp that reads Figma directly. The full loop:
      <strong>inspect → prompt → ship → iterate</strong>.
    </PageHero>

    <p
      style="
        font-size: var(--ui-font-size-sm);
        color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
        margin: -1.5rem 0 2rem;
        max-width: 620px;
        line-height: 1.6;
      "
    >
      An MCP server exposes a small set of functions an AI client can call over
      a standard request/response format (JSON-RPC — think "remote function
      call"); each Atelier UI MCP returns structured data, no screenshots or
      copy-paste.
    </p>

    <section
      aria-labelledby="your-servers"
      style="margin: 0 0 2.5rem; max-width: 820px"
    >
      <h2 id="your-servers" class="docs-h3">
        Your MCP servers in the workshop
      </h2>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        The workspace generator writes a <code>.mcp.json</code> at the root of
        your workspace. Here is what each entry is for in the loop
        (<strong>inspect → contract → code → verify</strong>), one thing to ask
        it, and how to tell it works. Which entries you have depends on your
        answers: <code>storybook-&lt;framework&gt;</code> is the framework you
        picked, <code>angular-cli</code> exists only for Angular, and
        <code>figma-console</code> only if you said yes to Figma. The exact file
        is on
        <a href="/workshop#mcp-config" class="docs-link-primary"
          >the workshop page</a
        >.
      </p>
      <div class="docs-table-scroll">
        <table class="docs-props-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Server</th>
              <th>What it does</th>
              <th>Loop step</th>
              <th>Try asking</th>
              <th>Working when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>storybook-&lt;framework&gt;</code></td>
              <td>
                Hosted, read-only component docs for your framework: props,
                variants, stories, usage. Only <code>docs-list</code>,
                <code>docs-show</code> and <code>docs-show-story</code>.
              </td>
              <td>Code (and the exact prop names the contract refers to)</td>
              <td>
                "Call <code>docs-list</code>, then <code>docs-show</code> for
                the button and tell me its variants."
              </td>
              <td>
                Claude lists real component IDs and prop names from the docs
                instead of guessing them.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>figma-console</code> (if you chose Figma)</td>
              <td>
                Reads (and can write) your Figma file through the Desktop Bridge
                plugin.
              </td>
              <td>Inspect, and the final parity check in Verify</td>
              <td>
                "Use the figma-console MCP to call <code>figma_get_status</code>
                and report what it returns."
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>figma_get_status</code> reports the bridge as connected
                (the plugin panel must be open in Figma Desktop). Setup:
                <a href="/figma-token" class="docs-link-primary">Figma Setup</a
                >.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>uianatomy</code></td>
              <td>
                Canonical component anatomy: axes, slots, states, tokens,
                events, and where the common libraries disagree.
              </td>
              <td>Contract: settle an ambiguous prop shape or interaction</td>
              <td>
                "Use uianatomy to show the anatomy of a modal (dialog): slots,
                states and keyboard behaviour."
              </td>
              <td>
                The answer names slots and states from uianatomy rather than
                from the model's memory.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>nx-mcp</code></td>
              <td>
                Workspace knowledge: projects, targets, generators and Nx docs.
                The Atelier preset does not set up Nx Cloud for your workspace,
                so the CI tools only work if you connect it yourself;
                <code>nx_docs</code> works either way.
              </td>
              <td>Code and Verify: find and run the right target</td>
              <td>
                "Use the Nx MCP's <code>nx_docs</code> to explain what
                <code>nx test</code> does."
              </td>
              <td>The server shows as connected in <code>/mcp</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>angular-cli</code> (Angular only)</td>
              <td>Angular best practices, API search and examples.</td>
              <td>Code</td>
              <td>
                "Use angular-cli to run <code>list_projects</code>, then load
                the best practices for this workspace."
              </td>
              <td>
                <code>list_projects</code> returns your workspace and its app.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65; font-size: var(--ui-font-size-sm)">
        The hosted Storybook server serves docs only. Tools that need a running
        Storybook (<code>stories-preview</code>, <code>test-run</code>) are not
        in your <code>.mcp.json</code>; see
        <a href="/workshop#mcp-config" class="docs-link-primary"
          >the workshop page</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Check it</h3>
      <ol style="line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.2rem">
        <li>
          Open the workspace folder in Claude Code and accept the "do you trust
          the files in this folder?" prompt. Until you do,
          <code>.mcp.json</code> is ignored.
        </li>
        <li>
          Run <code>/mcp</code> in the session. Every server from your
          <code>.mcp.json</code> should be listed as connected; approve any that
          ask for approval. A server listed as failed, or missing from the list,
          is the one to fix. The workshop's
          <a href="/workshop" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Open in Claude Code step</a
          >
          describes the same check.
        </li>
        <li>
          "Connected" only proves the server started or the URL answered. Run
          the "Try asking" prompt from the table for each server you care about.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        <strong>If one fails, first:</strong> restart Claude Code from the
        directory that contains <code>.mcp.json</code>, then run
        <code>npm run preflight</code>, which checks each hosted Storybook
        endpoint with a real MCP call. Details and curl checks:
        <a href="/troubleshooting#mcp-not-loaded" class="docs-link-primary"
          >config not picked up</a
        >,
        <a href="/troubleshooting#mcp-unreachable" class="docs-link-primary"
          >server unreachable</a
        >,
        <a href="/troubleshooting#mcp-tools-error" class="docs-link-primary"
          >connects but tools error</a
        >. For <code>figma-console</code>, see
        <a
          href="/troubleshooting#figma-bridge-plugin-not-connected"
          class="docs-link-primary"
          >bridge plugin not connected</a
        >
        and <a href="/figma-token" class="docs-link-primary">Figma Setup</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Next: your own design system</h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        These servers serve Atelier's components. For your own design system the
        same pattern applies: see
        <a href="#own-mcp" class="docs-link-primary"
          >Publish your own design-system MCP</a
        >
        below for the cheapest way to get there.
      </p>
    </section>

    <MaintainerOnly>
      <h2 id="for-maintainers" class="docs-h3" style="margin: 2.5rem 0 0.5rem">
        For maintainers
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
          max-width: 720px;
          line-height: 1.65;
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
        "
      >
        Everything below is about how this repo and its hosted servers are
        built. You do not need it to do the workshop, apart from the last
        section on publishing your own server.
      </p>

      <aside
        aria-label="Storybook MCP tool surface at a glance"
        style="
          margin: -0.5rem 0 1.25rem;
          padding: 0.95rem 1.15rem;
          border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border);
          border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md);
          background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised);
          line-height: 1.65;
          max-width: 720px;
        "
      >
        <strong>Storybook MCP at a glance.</strong> Three toolsets —
        <code>docs</code> (<code>docs-list</code>, <code>docs-show</code>,
        <code>docs-show-story</code>), <code>dev</code>
        (<code>get-storybook-story-instructions</code>,
        <code>stories-preview</code>, <code>stories-changed</code>,
        <code>stories-find-by-component</code>, <code>review-create</code>),
        <code>test</code> (<code>test-run</code>). Hosted MCPs ship the
        <code>docs</code> toolset only; <code>dev</code> + <code>test</code>
        live in <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> behind a running Storybook.
        Those tool names are the ones Atelier's pinned versions ship — Storybook
        10.6.0, <code>@storybook/mcp</code> 10.6.0,
        <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> 10.6.0 — read from the installed
        <code>dist/preset.js</code>, not the changelog prose, which still
        narrates <code>review-create</code> as "the display-review tool". See
        <a href="/storybook/#toolset-configuration" class="docs-link-primary"
          >Storybook → Toolset configuration</a
        >
        for the full matrix, what 10.4 added
        (<code>experimentalReactComponentMeta</code> for React's docgen, now
        joined by Angular's and Vue's own <code>experimentalDocgenServer</code>;
        Change Review, MCP Apps preview, multi-source composition, self-host
        API), and gating rules.
      </aside>

      <aside
        aria-label="Wired vs. not wired, and the cost to close the gap"
        style="
          margin: 0 0 2.25rem;
          padding: 0.95rem 1.15rem;
          border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border);
          border-radius: var(--ui-radius-md);
          background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised);
          line-height: 1.65;
          max-width: 720px;
        "
      >
        <strong>Wired vs. not, and what closing the gap costs.</strong>
        A scaffolded workspace's <code>.mcp.json</code> wires the one hosted
        Storybook endpoint for your framework (plus <code>nx-mcp</code>,
        <code>uianatomy</code>, <code>angular-cli</code> for Angular and
        <code>figma-console</code> if chosen — see
        <a href="/workshop#mcp-config" class="docs-link-primary"
          >the workshop page</a
        >) — none of them exposes the three <code>dev</code>-toolset tools that
        need a running Storybook (<code>stories-changed</code>,
        <code>stories-find-by-component</code>, <code>review-create</code>);
        only a local entry pointed at
        <code>http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;/mcp</code> does, and that stays a
        per-developer choice on purpose, not something this repo wires for
        everyone.
        <ul style="margin: 0.6rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.2rem">
          <li>
            <code>stories-changed</code> and
            <code>stories-find-by-component</code> already cost nothing beyond
            that local entry: <code>features.changeDetection</code> defaults to
            <code>true</code> in 10.6 core and none of the three
            <code>main.ts</code> files override it, and all three frameworks
            build on <code>builder-vite</code>, which implements the
            module-graph adapter <code>stories-find-by-component</code> needs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>review-create</code> needs one more step: for a direct MCP
            client (a local entry, not the <code>storybook ai</code> CLI
            channel) it registers only once
            <code>features.experimentalReview: true</code> is set explicitly —
            unset today in all three <code>main.ts</code> files. Three one-line
            config additions, no new dependency; "not set to <code>false</code>"
            is enough only on the CLI channel below, not here.
          </li>
          <li>
            <code>storybook ai</code> — the CLI channel whose requests get
            <code>review-create</code> without the flag — still ships in 10.6.0,
            but its own <code>--help</code> now prints a deprecation notice
            pointing at <code>storybook skills</code>. This repo already moved:
            the <code>stories</code>, <code>storybook-init</code>,
            <code>storybook-setup</code> and <code>storybook-upgrade</code>
            Claude Code Skills came from <code>storybook skills setup</code>
            (ADR-0123) — a one-time file install, unrelated to the MCP servers
            on this page.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <DiagramFigure
