      </DiagramFigure>

      <section
        aria-labelledby="figma-servers-heading"
        style="margin: 2.25rem 0 2rem; max-width: 820px"
      >
        <h2 id="figma-servers-heading" class="docs-h3">
          figma-console-mcp vs. Figma's official MCP server
        </h2>
        <p style="line-height: 1.65">
          If you already know Figma's own MCP server (the "Dev Mode MCP"), this
          is how it differs from the <code>figma-console-mcp</code> that Atelier
          wires in <code>.mcp.json</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="docs-table-scroll">
          <table class="docs-props-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Dimension</th>
                <th>Figma's official MCP server</th>
                <th><code>figma-console-mcp</code></th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>What it is</td>
                <td>Figma's own server; you point it at a node with a link</td>
                <td>
                  Open-source server that drives a plugin inside Figma Desktop
                  (the Desktop Bridge)
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Read vs. write</td>
                <td>
                  Started read-only; since early 2026 the remote server edits
                  the canvas too (<code>use_figma</code>: frames, components,
                  variables). The local desktop server stays read-only
                </td>
                <td>
                  Read and write through the Plugin API, including component
                  sets and variables
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>What comes back</td>
                <td>
                  Opinionated code, in the demos React with Tailwind classes,
                  for the agent to translate into your stack
                </td>
                <td>
                  Structured data about the component, plus the plugin console
                  logs
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Variables / tokens</td>
                <td>
                  <code>get_variable_defs</code> returns the variables used by a
                  selection, for the active mode; names come back raw when a
                  variable has no code syntax set
                </td>
                <td>
                  Variables and token tools (<code>figma_get_variables</code>,
                  <code>figma_browse_tokens</code>) that resolve aliases across
                  all modes, on any Figma plan
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Tools in the Atelier loop</td>
                <td>None</td>
                <td>
                  <code>figma_get_component_for_development</code>,
                  <code>figma_check_design_parity</code>,
                  <code>figma_scan_code_accessibility</code>
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Setup</td>
                <td>
                  Remote server with OAuth, on every plan (free seats get very
                  few calls); the desktop server needs a paid Dev or Full seat
                </td>
                <td>
                  <code>npx</code> entry in <code>.mcp.json</code> (version
                  pinned there, ADR-0110) with a <code>FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN</code
                  >, plus the Desktop Bridge plugin
                </td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p style="line-height: 1.65; font-size: var(--ui-font-size-sm)">
          <strong>Dated and perishable.</strong> This reflects mid-2026; both
          servers change quickly, so check their docs before relying on a row.
          Atelier chose <code>figma-console-mcp</code> when the official server
          was still read-mostly (ADR-0111). That gap has narrowed since; what
          still decides it here are the loop's own tools above — parity, the
          accessibility scan and multi-mode variables.
        </p>
      </section>
    </MaintainerOnly>

    <section
      aria-labelledby="own-mcp"
      style="margin: 2.25rem 0 2rem; max-width: 820px"
    >
      <h2 id="own-mcp" class="docs-h3">Publish your own design-system MCP</h2>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        Everything above is Atelier's servers. For your own design system the
        question is how little you have to build. Start at the cheap end and
        move right only when a concrete need pushes you.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">
        What a server gives an agent that a docs file does not
      </h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        A static <code>llms.txt</code> (Atelier serves
        <a href="/llms.txt" class="docs-link-primary"><code>/llms.txt</code></a>
        and <code>/llms-full.txt</code>) is a file the agent reads whole or
        fetches by URL. An MCP server answers a question: the agent calls
        <code>docs-list</code>, then <code>docs-show</code> for one component,
        and gets only that component's props, stories and usage. So
        <code>llms.txt</code> is enough while your design system is small enough
        to sit in the context window and you have no need for per-component
        lookups; a server pays off when the full text is too big to load every
        time, or when you want the same answers inside every client without
        pasting files.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">
        Cheapest path: static manifests behind Storybook's MCP handler
      </h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        If your design system already has a Storybook, you build no server
        logic. Atelier's hosted endpoints
        (<code>atelier.pieper.io/storybook-{'{angular,react,vue}'}/mcp</code>)
        are one small Cloudflare Worker that hands Storybook's own handler the
        static files a Storybook build already wrote. Steps to repeat:
      </p>
      <ol style="line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.2rem">
        <li>
          <strong>Make Storybook emit a manifest.</strong> Atelier's three
          <code>main.ts</code> files set
          <code>features.componentsManifest: true</code> and add
          <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code>; Angular and Vue also set
          <code>experimentalDocgenServer: true</code>, React uses
          <code>react-docgen</code> by default (ADR-0097). A build then writes
          <code>manifests/components.json</code> and
          <code>manifests/docs.json</code> into its output directory.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Build it as static output.</strong> Atelier runs
          <code
            >storybook build --config-dir &lt;lib&gt;/.storybook --output-dir
            dist/docs/storybook-&lt;fw&gt;</code
          >
          (the exact command is the <code>build.command</code> in
          <code>wrangler.jsonc</code>), so the manifests sit next to the
          rendered stories.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Serve it with <code>@storybook/mcp</code>.</strong>
          <code>worker/mcp.ts</code> calls
          <code>createStorybookMcpHandler</code> with a
          <code>manifestProvider</code> that reads the requested manifest path
          from the Worker's static-assets binding, and
          <code>worker/index.ts</code> routes
          <code>/storybook-&lt;fw&gt;/mcp</code> to it. Copy those two files as
          your template. Read the comments in <code>worker/mcp.ts</code> and
          <code>wrangler.jsonc</code> before you copy: the manifests are fetched
          through the binding, not by public URL (a Worker calling its own zone
          fails), and the <code>define</code> entry works around the
          <code>@storybook/mcp@10.6.0</code> module preamble in a Worker.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guard the artefact.</strong> A manifest with zero components
          makes <code>@storybook/mcp</code> throw, so
          <code>check:storybook-manifests</code> builds all three Storybooks and
          checks the files offline.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deploy.</strong> Atelier's deploy is a Cloudflare build that
          runs the <code>build.command</code> above; CI builds the same
          Storybooks so a broken deploy shows up in a pull request
          (<code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>). You do not need Cloudflare
          for this: the course demo hosted its server on Netlify Functions.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        Pins that Atelier uses today: Storybook, <code>@storybook/mcp</code> and
        <code>@storybook/addon-mcp</code> all at <code>10.6.0</code>. Because
        the handler consumes static files, the tool surface is <code>docs</code>
        only; <code>dev</code> and <code>test</code> need a running Storybook
        (see above).
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Connect it from a client</h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        Claude Code reads a remote server from <code>.mcp.json</code>. This is
        how Atelier wires its own:
      </p>
      <pre style="overflow-x: auto"><code>{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "storybook-angular": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://atelier.pieper.io/storybook-angular/mcp"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        Per the vendors' docs (checked 2026-10-09): Codex takes
        <code>[mcp_servers.&lt;name&gt;]</code> with a <code>url</code> key in
        <code>config.toml</code>, or
        <code>codex mcp add &lt;name&gt; --url &lt;url&gt;</code>
        (<a
          href="https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/extend/mcp?surface=cli"
          class="docs-link-primary"
          >Codex MCP docs</a
        >). Cursor takes a <code>url</code> entry under <code>mcpServers</code>
        in <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>
        (<a href="https://cursor.com/docs/context/mcp" class="docs-link-primary"
          >Cursor MCP docs</a
        >). Client settings change, so use those pages as the reference.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">
        The expensive end: a custom server with a knowledge graph
      </h3>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        The design-systems course goes further. Its Eddie demo had a local stdio
        server reading a knowledge graph of component information from disk,
        with eight tools. To publish it the demo refactored the server to run
        remotely, added a streamable HTTP entry point, bundled the graph into
        the deployment, wrapped it as a Netlify function and added a way to test
        the tools over HTTP locally. It then connected from Codex as a
        streamable-HTTP server and asked for a card component's props,
        composition and usage guidance; the answer also surfaced a gap in the
        graph (missing relationship metadata). The course itself says this is
        specific to that setup, not generic advice.
      </p>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        Build that when the answers you need are not in a component manifest:
        relationships between components, page patterns, composition rules. What
        it costs is a server you own, a graph you keep current, and its own tool
        design and tests. The course also stresses that techniques for agent
        access are still emerging, so expect to revisit it.
      </p>
      <p style="line-height: 1.65">
        <strong>One rule either way:</strong> a public docs MCP should serve
        only public docs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <McpExplorer client:load />
  </div>
